      "objective": "Independently re-review (round 3, after the author addressed round-2 findings) this proposed change to a campaign execution prompt (the candidate is a unified diff adding section 19A). The prompt's own rules for such changes are in evidence id prompt-s18. Check: contradictions with the rest of the prompt; whether any line weakens or bypasses public-interface use, independent review, evidence, acceptance or merge gates, the pause-and-help rule, the no-local-LLM rule, isolation, or protections for human work; whether it grants new permissions or spending; recovery gaps; unnecessary burden; vague or untestable wording; and whether each lesson's cited trigger justifies it. Use ASSUMPTION lines for problems, CITE for evidence ids, and IDEA only for a concrete wording fix. Evidence id r001-journal holds the journal entries the triggers cite. Report only BLOCKING problems (a contradiction, a bypass of a gate, a new permission, or an untestable instruction); list anything else as minor.",
      "text": "CITE: prompt-s18\nCITE: r001-journal\n\nASSUMPTION: BLOCKING (new permission, unsupported number). Bullet 2 says the user approved \"at most 5 per table\" of short attributed examples. Nothing in r001-journal records that approval. The 04:45Z entry says only that bulk records must not be published. An invented cap would grant a new publication permission, which s18 forbids (\"Grant new permissions\") and which the bullet's own \"never infer\" rule bars.\nUNKNOWN: user_approval_of_five_examples_per_table\nIDEA: Replace \"(R001: at most 5 per table)\" with \"only a number of examples the user has approved in a dated, journaled instruction; if none is journaled, the number is zero.\"\n\nASSUMPTION: BLOCKING (new permission, protected state). Bullet 4 lets a controller run an unmerged, unreviewed engine candidate through the public CLI with PYTHONPATH and then \"discard any scratch sessions it creates\". No journal trigger supports this. The 17:45Z entry covers only the regression gate and the timeout. Running unmerged code on live campaign sessions is a new capability. Discarding sessions edits runtime state, which s18 protects, and it could hide consumed HowlPlane session counts (s18: \"reset consumed budgets\"). The \"advisory only\" caveat does not remove the permission.\nIDEA: Delete the PYTHONPATH sentence. Alternatively, limit it to a throwaway repo, state that session counts stay consumed and are recorded, and forbid discarding sessions.\n\nASSUMPTION: BLOCKING (weakens pause-and-help, untestable). Bullet 5 lets the controller recover readiness and continue once a downgrade \"counts as traced\". \"Identified from session or CLI evidence\" is not testable. The journal shows the campaign went to PAUSED_NEEDS_HELP on the CUBS-P-002 and CUBS-P-007 Howl failures (17:45Z, 23:35Z). The bullet turns those pauses into self-recovery, which bypasses the s17 pause-and-help rule.\nIDEA: Say that a Howl-caused downgrade is still recorded as a help incident and paused under section 17. Only after that may the user-approved documented recovery command be run.\n\nASSUMPTION: BLOCKING (unverified command, possible spending). The example `howlplane agents doctor --live --agent <agent> --repo <repo>` appears nowhere in the evidence. \"--live\" may make real agent calls that use quota or spend. That would be a spending or permission increase, and a quota-exhausted agent (CUBS-P-007) would simply fail again.\nUNKNOWN: howlplane_agents_doctor_live_semantics_and_cost\n\nASSUMPTION: BLOCKING (untestable bound, possible budget evasion). Bullet 7 says to re-judge \"within the existing rework bounds\". The journal's recovery path (15:35Z) discards S3 and starts a new session. A new session restarts the \"Rework rounds: n of 2\" counter and consumes a session slot (14 of 16). So the bound cannot be checked, and the path can be used to get past an exhausted rework limit.\nIDEA: Add that a replacement session counts toward the session budget, that rework rounds carried over from the discarded session are recorded, and that the total rework budget is not reset.\n\nASSUMPTION: Minor. Bullet 4's \"at least twice its last observed runtime\" gives 912 s from the observed 456 s. The journal says implementers time out at 600 s (CUBS-P-004). It is unknown whether --verify-timeout also governs those roles.\nASSUMPTION: Minor. Bullet 3 embeds engine internals (the clamp formula) that will go stale. \"Verify against the engine\" is acceptable, but the formula should be marked as an example.\nASSUMPTION: Minor. Bullet 6 says to discard a mission-owned session. It should say explicitly that the discard consumes no budget and that the preserved patch hash is recorded.\nASSUMPTION: Minor. Bullet 1 is justified by 09:35Z. Its text \"if it is not yet in the journal, record it there\" is fine.\nASSUMPTION: Minor. The 04:45Z journal entry has a stray timestamp (\"== 2026-10-09T12:37:15Z\"), so its ordering is ambiguous as a trigger.",
